<BaseLayout
  title="Profile | Masahiro Sadamura — Legal Psychology, The University of Osaka"
  description="Profile of Masahiro Sadamura. Legal psychology, Graduate School of Human Sciences, The University of Osaka."
>
  <h1>Profile</h1>

  <h2>Name</h2>
  <p>Masahiro SADAMURA (<span lang="ja">貞村 真宏 / さだむら まさひろ</span>)</p>

  <h2>Current Position</h2>
  <p>
    Researcher at the Graduate School of Human Sciences, The University of Osaka.
    J.D., The University of Tokyo.
  </p>

  <h2>Brief Biographical Statement</h2>
  <p>
    Masahiro Sadamura is a researcher in legal psychology at the Graduate
    School of Human Sciences, The University of Osaka. His research examines
    how criminal courts handle welfare-related information about defendants —
    developmental adversity, disability, social isolation — and what role such
    information should play where criminal justice meets social welfare.
  </p>
  <p>
    He has published 21 peer-reviewed articles in psychology and law journals
    (as of August 2026) and presented at international meetings, including the
    annual conference of the European Society of Criminology. Methodologically,
    he combines vignette experiments and path analysis with natural language
    processing of Japanese sentencing judgments and explainable machine
    learning (SHAP), building evidence at the level of both the individual
    judgment and the system as a whole.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bridging law and social psychology, he works toward an evidence-based
    account of how welfare considerations can, and should, enter sentencing in
    Japanese criminal justice. His research has been recognized with awards
    from the Japan Society for Research on Emotions, the Japan Society of
    Personality Psychology, and the Japanese Society for Law and Psychology.
  </p>

  <h2>Research Areas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Legal psychology (the intersection of law and psychology)</li>
    <li>The role of welfare-oriented support information in sentencing decisions</li>
    <li>Analysis of judicial sentencing texts using natural language processing and explainable AI (SHAP)</li>
    <li>Mixed-methods research (vignette experiments + path analysis + machine learning)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Research Keywords</h2>
  <p>
    Focal concerns theory · Lay judge system · Elderly offenders · Redeemability ·
    Welfare-oriented support · Sentencing decisions · Criminal justice ·
    Explainable AI (SHAP) · Machine learning · Natural language processing ·
    Law and psychology
  </p>

